---
/* ============================================================================
   /v2b — My Portfolio v2B.
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A parallel art direction for the same brand: the mesh portrait as the whole
   hero, then the work as an index. v2 is untouched and still lives at /.

   THE HERO COMPOSITION WAS PORTED FROM hero-parallax_6.html — the cowork
   session's sixth iteration — and has since been recomposed. The port was a
   centred portrait with the UI overlaid: eyebrow and headline bottom-left,
   CTA bottom-right, vertical meta up the right edge.

   WHY THAT COMPOSITION WAS REPLACED. Measured at 1440x900 it put every text
   element between y=495 and y=752 of an 808px section, left 61% of the hero
   as empty ground, and clamped the headline to a 355px column that broke it
   across three lines at 48px — while looking correct in a screenshot of the
   top and correct again on a phone, which is why it survived several passes.
   A centred subject is also the most conventional placement available, and it
   was fighting the asymmetric bottom-left / bottom-right UI rather than
   supporting it.

   THE HERO IS NOW AN ASYMMETRIC SPLIT: the portrait is anchored to the right
   and bleeds 2rem past the container, the type owns a 34rem column on the
   left and is vertically centred, and the scrim runs left-to-right instead of
   bottom-up. Mobile still stacks, and the old arrangement is restored there.
   See the notes on .mesh-hero__layer and .mesh-hero__scrim.

   WHAT CHANGED IN THE PORT, and why:

   1. NO RUNTIME TRIANGULATION. An earlier pass here built a component that
      Delaunay-triangulated a photograph in the browser. That was the right
      answer to the wrong question: this asset is ALREADY a mesh render, and
      re-triangulating it would only blur topology that is crisper in the
      source. The image is a layer that moves. src/components/v2b/PolyMesh.astro
      still exists and is no longer imported by anything.

   2. THE RECT IS CACHED. The original read getBoundingClientRect() inside the
      pointermove handler, which forces a synchronous layout on every single
      move event — many times per frame. It is now read once on enter and
      refreshed on resize and scroll.

   3. THE LOOP IS GATED BY AN IntersectionObserver, so it cannot run while the
      hero is off screen. Same contract DotGrid and the rest of this codebase
      hold.

   4. THE PULSING DOT IS STATIC. The original ran `animation: ping 2.4s
      infinite`. src/scripts/motion.ts documents, at length, that this site has
      no Pause/Stop/Hide control precisely BECAUSE nothing loops forever — a
      single infinite animation reinstates WCAG 2.2.2 (Level A) and forces a
      pause control back into the header. A status dot is not worth that, so it
      is a plain dot. The entry reveal added during the recomposition is a
      one-shot that finishes in 900ms and so does not reopen that question;
      the note next to @keyframes mesh-rise spells the distinction out.

   5. PALETTE IS COBALT, NOT LIME. The cowork file uses #d9ff5a on #242424.
      That is a genuine fork from the locked cobalt palette, and it is not mine
      to make silently — see the note at --mesh-accent below for how to try it.

   THE WORK IS A LIST, NOT A GRID — the other big departure from v2. v2 shows
   work as filled cards with filter chips, tag pills and a summary each. Here
   it is an index: number, title, discipline, and the cover only when you point
   at a row. The grid says "look at the pictures"; the index says "read the
   list, then look". Judging that trade is the point of building it.
   ========================================================================= */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { getImage, Image } from 'astro:assets';
import V2BLayout from '../../layouts/V2BLayout.astro';
import { PERSON } from '../../config/site';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';

/* The real asset, extracted from hero-parallax_6.html where it was a base64
   data URI. 726x1166, transparent WebP. The side blur on hair, ears and neck
   and the fade to nothing at the edges are BAKED INTO THE FILE — do not try to
   reproduce them in CSS, and do not put a background behind this layer. */
import meshPortrait from '../../assets/jayson-mesh-headshot.webp';

const entries = (await getCollection('work', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort((a, b) =>
  a.data.order !== b.data.order
    ? a.data.order - b.data.order
    : a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title),
);

/* Small, because these are hover thumbnails and never appear at size. */
const rows = await Promise.all(
  entries.map(async (entry) => ({
    id: entry.id,
    title: entry.data.shortTitle ?? entry.data.title,
    category: entry.data.category,
    preview: await getImage({ src: entry.data.cover, width: 420, format: 'webp' }),
  })),
);

---

<V2BLayout title="v2B">
  {/* -- Hero ------------------------------------------------------------ */}
  <section class="mesh-hero" id="mesh-hero" aria-labelledby="v2b-hero-heading">
    {/* The only thing that moves. aria-hidden: the portrait is atmosphere and
        the <h1> below already names whose page this is — a screen reader
        announcing a wireframe render adds nothing. */}
    <div class="mesh-hero__layer" data-mesh-layer aria-hidden="true">
      <Image
        class="mesh-hero__img"
        src={meshPortrait}
        alt=""
        widths={[480, 726]}
        sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 46rem, 100vw"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>

    {/* Protects the bottom UI from the portrait. Measured, not eyeballed —
        without it the status line sat at 1.49:1 over the lit side of the
        face. See the note on .mesh-hero__scrim. */}
    <div class="mesh-hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="mesh-hero__ui v2b-container">
      <div class="mesh-hero__intro">
        <p class="mesh-hero__status">
          {/* Static. See note 4 in the header. */}
          <span class="mesh-hero__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          Available for projects
        </p>

        {/* THE HEADLINE NAMES THE JOB. The line it replaced — "Designing
            digital form with structure & feeling" — was a mood, and a visitor
            deciding in the two or three minutes a portfolio actually gets
            cannot buy a mood. The rare thing here is that the strategy and the
            craft come from one person, so the headline says that, and the
            serif clause carries the feeling the old line was reaching for.

            The <em> is display:block rather than left to wrap, because
            text-wrap: balance would happily break this as "Marketing strategy
            and / the design to carry it" and put the family change mid-line.
            An explicit break is deterministic at every width. */}
        <h1 class="mesh-hero__title" id="v2b-hero-heading">
          Marketing strategy <em>and the design to carry it</em>
        </h1>

        <p class="mesh-hero__who">
          {PERSON.name}. {PERSON.tagline} in {PERSON.location.city}.
        </p>

        <div class="mesh-hero__actions">
          <a class="mesh-hero__cta" href="#work">
            View selected work
            <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          {/* One label per intent: the header, this row and any future contact
              section all say "Contact", never "Get in touch" or "Let's talk".
              The hero is where the decision to make contact actually gets
              made, and the address should not be a treasure hunt. */}
          <a class="mesh-hero__contact" href={`mailto:${PERSON.email}`}>Contact</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* -- Work index ------------------------------------------------------ */}
  <section class="v2b-section v2b-container" id="work" aria-labelledby="v2b-work-heading">
    {/* A real heading, not a styled <p>: this is what aria-labelledby points
        at, and the count moves into its own cell rather than hanging off an
        em-dash. v2b.css lays the two out as a baseline-aligned row. */}
    <h2 class="v2b-eyebrow" id="v2b-work-heading">
      <span>Work</span>
      <span>{rows.length} projects</span>
    </h2>

    <ul class="index" role="list" data-index>
      {
        rows.map((row, i) => (
          <li class="index__row">
            <a class="index__link" href={href(`/work/${row.id}/`)} data-preview={row.preview.src}>
              {/* Decorative ordering. The row is already a list item and
                  announcing "zero one" before every title is noise. */}
              <span class="index__n" aria-hidden="true">
                {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
              </span>
              <span class="index__title">{row.title}</span>
              <span class="index__cat">{row.category}</span>
              <span class="index__arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                &rarr;
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    {/* Follows the pointer. aria-hidden and empty until script fills it. */}
    <div class="index__preview" data-preview-pane aria-hidden="true">
      <img alt="" decoding="async" />
    </div>
  </section>
</V2BLayout>

<style>
  /* -- Hero --------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .mesh-hero {
    /* --mx/--my are the eased pointer position, -1..1, written by script.
       Declared here so the very first paint is centred rather than unset. */
    --mx: 0;
    --my: 0;
    /* ONE place to retune the parallax. Raised from 24/16: a centred subject
       reads its own displacement against the frame, an off-centre one does
       not, so the same amplitude that was legible in the symmetric
       composition measured as roughly 1.7% of the hero width and simply
       disappeared. Still small enough that the crop never reveals an edge. */
    --push-x: 40px;
    --push-y: 22px;
    /* Where the portrait's head sits, for the halo below. The stacked
       composition centres it; the split block moves it right. */
    --head-x: 50%;
    --head-y: 30%;

    position: relative;
    /* svh, not vh: on mobile vh is the LARGEST viewport height, so a 100vh
       hero is taller than the visible area until the URL bar retracts.

       MINUS THE HEADER, because this one is not sticky and so occupies real
       flow above the hero. At a flat 100svh the section ended at 982px in a
       900px viewport and the CTA sat 26px below the fold. --v2b-header-h is
       measured and published by V2BLayout; the 5rem fallback only applies for
       the instant before its script runs. */
    min-block-size: calc(100svh - var(--v2b-header-h, 5rem));
    display: grid;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* No background. The portrait's edge fade is baked into its alpha and
       needs the page ground behind it, not a surface of its own. */
  }

  /* THE HALO. On the night ground the portrait's dark hair has the same
     luminance as #0a0b10 and the silhouette simply stops existing above the
     ears — which is why light mode always read as the stronger of the two
     even though dark is what an OS-dark visitor sees first. A wide, very soft
     lift behind the head restores the separation without becoming a visible
     shape. --mesh-halo is transparent on paper, where the head already
     separates; see v2b.css for the three-state definition.

     A ::before rather than a negative z-index element, deliberately. This
     pseudo-element and .mesh-hero__layer are both position:absolute with
     z-index:auto, so they paint in order and the halo lands underneath the
     portrait with nothing to isolate and nothing to stack. */
  .mesh-hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: radial-gradient(
      44% 48% at var(--head-x) var(--head-y),
      var(--mesh-halo) 0%,
      transparent 70%
    );
  }

  /* ABSOLUTE, not a grid item. As a grid item its 104%-tall image sized the
     row, so the hero grew to the image's height (1203px) instead of staying
     at 100svh — which pushed the headline and CTA below the fold on a 900px
     viewport while looking perfectly fine in a screenshot of the top. Taking
     it out of flow lets the section keep its own height and crop the
     portrait, which was the intent.

     THE BASE IS THE STACKED COMPOSITION: portrait centred at the top, type
     beneath it. The split is opt-in from 64rem up, in the block near the
     bottom of this file, because it genuinely does not fit below that — see
     the arithmetic there. */
  .mesh-hero__layer {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding-block-start: var(--space-l);
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Promotes the layer once, up front, rather than on the first frame of
       movement — which is where the visible hitch would otherwise be. */
    will-change: transform;
    transform: translate3d(
      calc(var(--mx) * var(--push-x)),
      calc(var(--my) * var(--push-y)),
      0
    );
  }

  .mesh-hero__img {
    inline-size: auto;
    block-size: 66%;
    max-inline-size: none;
  }

  /* A wash of the page's OWN ground. Same technique the v2 hero used to rescue
     its gradient: put the background back over the decoration exactly where
     text has to be read, and nowhere else.

     While the composition is stacked it runs bottom to top, putting the ground
     back under the type. The split block below swaps it to left-to-right,
     because once the type moves into its own column the only thing it can
     collide with is the portrait's faded left tail, and a bottom-up scrim
     would wash out the neck for no reason while leaving that overlap
     unprotected. */
  .mesh-hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      var(--bg) 0%,
      var(--bg) 40%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 72%, transparent) 66%,
      transparent 100%
    );
  }

  /* A browser without color-mix would drop the whole gradient declaration and
     leave the text unprotected, so the fallback is a plain three-stop ramp. */
  @supports not (background: color-mix(in srgb, red 50%, transparent)) {
    .mesh-hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(to top, var(--bg) 0%, var(--bg) 46%, transparent 100%);
    }
  }

  .mesh-hero__ui {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    align-content: end;
    padding-block: var(--space-xl);
  }

  /* 34rem, not 34ch. The old ch cap resolved against the INTRO's font-size
     rather than the headline's, which pinned the h1 to 355px and broke it
     across three lines at 48px while looking fine on a phone. A rem cap is
     independent of which element inside is being measured, and 34rem leaves
     clearance before the portrait's faded left edge at 1440. */
  .mesh-hero__intro {
    max-inline-size: min(100%, 35rem);
  }

  /* ====================================================================
     THE SPLIT. Opt-in from 64rem, and the breakpoint is arithmetic rather
     than a round number picked by feel.

     The split needs textColumn + gap + portraitWidth to fit inside the
     viewport. The text column caps at 35rem (560px) and the portrait was
     534px wide at 1440x900, which needs roughly 1120px of content before
     gutters. Left at the old 42rem (672px) fallback, an 800px tablet put
     the text column at 32..592 and the portrait's left edge at 266 — the
     headline would have sat across the middle of the face. 64rem is the
     first width where the arithmetic clears, given the vw cap below.
     ==================================================================== */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .mesh-hero {
      --head-x: 72%;
      --head-y: 38%;
    }

    /* Right-anchored. Centred, the portrait owned the middle of a 1425px
       hero, all four text elements were crushed into the bottom 313px of an
       808px section, and 61% of the hero was empty ground. Pushing the
       subject into the right half turns that dead space into a real column.

       inset-inline-end lands the right edge 2rem PAST the container's edge,
       so the portrait reads as cropped by the viewport rather than floating
       in it. max(0px, ...) collapses the offset where the container is
       already full-bleed instead of pushing the subject off screen. */
    .mesh-hero__layer {
      inset-inline-start: auto;
      inset-inline-end: max(0px, calc(50% - var(--v2b-max) / 2 - 2rem));
      justify-content: flex-end;
      align-items: flex-end;
      padding-block-start: 0;
    }

    /* 104% of the hero crops the portrait, which is what stops the parallax
       from ever revealing an edge — but hero height tracks the VIEWPORT
       HEIGHT, and the thing the portrait must not collide with is the text
       column, which tracks viewport WIDTH. On a 1440x1200 window the
       percentage alone made the portrait 727px wide and ran it 168px into
       the headline. The 59vw cap is the same 534px width the composition was
       tuned at, expressed against the axis that actually constrains it. */
    .mesh-hero__img {
      block-size: min(104%, 59vw);
      margin-block-end: -2%;
    }

    .mesh-hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(
        to right,
        var(--bg) 0%,
        var(--bg) 30%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 70%, transparent) 46%,
        transparent 60%
      );
    }

    @supports not (background: color-mix(in srgb, red 50%, transparent)) {
      .mesh-hero__scrim {
        background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--bg) 0%, var(--bg) 36%, transparent 60%);
      }
    }

    /* Vertically centred, not bottom-pinned. Bottom-pinning is what stacked
       every element into the last 39% of the section in the first place. */
    .mesh-hero__ui {
      align-content: center;
    }
  }

  .mesh-hero__status {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-s);
  }

  .mesh-hero__dot {
    inline-size: 7px;
    block-size: 7px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--accent);
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* Lighter than v2's 700. The portrait is still the loud element and a heavy
     headline makes two things shouting.

     text-wrap: normal overrides base.css's `balance`. The two lines are set
     explicitly by the em's display:block below, and balance applied on top of
     that only redistributes words WITHIN each line for no gain — and would
     fight the measured fit of the serif line, which is the wider of the two.

     The clamp ceiling is set by the serif clause, not the sans line. At 3.1rem
     in a 34rem column the italic measured 544px inside 544px — it fit, but
     with zero slack, so any fallback face or rounding difference would have
     dropped it to a second line and made the headline three lines again. 3rem
     against the 35rem column below leaves about 33px, which is enough to
     absorb that without shrinking the display type meaningfully. */
  .mesh-hero__title {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.2rem + 3vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    text-wrap: normal;
    margin-block-end: var(--space-s);
  }

  /* base.css styles h1 em as Instrument Serif italic in --accent, and here it
     KEEPS the accent. The old override dimmed it to --text-dim because the
     clause sat over the lit side of the face; in the split it sits on clean
     ground, so the one accent-coloured element in the composition can finally
     do its job. Contrast is 8.2:1 on paper and 7.3:1 on night, both well past
     the 3:1 large-text floor.

     line-height and the padding reserve are not cosmetic: "design" and
     "carry" carry g and y descenders, and an italic descender at 1.05 clips
     against the next box. */
  .mesh-hero__title em {
    display: block;
    line-height: 1.15;
    padding-block-end: 0.08em;
  }

  /* --step-0, not --step--1. This is the line that names the role and the
     city, and it is what a visitor scanning for thirty seconds is actually
     looking for. At the smaller step it read as a caption on the headline
     rather than as the second most important sentence in the hero. */
  .mesh-hero__who {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-l);
  }

  .mesh-hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  /* A text link, not a second button. Two filled pills side by side would read
     as two equally weighted choices, and they are not: the work is what a
     visitor came for, contact is what they do after. */
  .mesh-hero__contact {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  .mesh-hero__contact:hover,
  .mesh-hero__contact:focus-visible {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.35em;
  }

  .mesh-hero__cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-l);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--text);
    color: var(--bg);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    white-space: nowrap;
    transition:
      transform var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out-quart),
      background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart),
      color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  .mesh-hero__cta:hover,
  .mesh-hero__cta:focus-visible {
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--on-accent);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  /* In forced-colors the fill is discarded and the button loses its only
     affordance — same fix v2's buttons carry. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .mesh-hero__cta {
      border-color: ButtonText;
    }
  }

  /* THE VERTICAL "PORTFOLIO / V2B / 2026" LABEL USED TO LIVE HERE, rotated up
     the right edge with physical inset properties because logical ones
     resolve against the element's own vertical-rl writing mode and put it
     through the middle of the face.

     It is deleted rather than fixed. Rotated edge type is agency-portfolio
     decoration: it told a visitor nothing they needed, it put a fifth text
     element in a hero that should carry four, and its right edge sat 25px
     from the CTA's. The year binding that fed it went with it. */

  /* THE ENTRY REVEAL. One staged fade-and-rise on load, and the only thing on
     this page that animates without the visitor causing it.

     It does NOT reinstate WCAG 2.2.2, and the distinction is the whole reason
     it is allowed where the old pulsing status dot was not: 2.2.2 governs
     motion that moves, blinks or scrolls automatically for MORE than five
     seconds. This finishes in 900ms and never repeats, so there is still
     nothing for a pause control to pause.

     CSS, not script, so it costs nothing and cannot fail half-applied. Gated
     behind no-preference rather than overridden under reduce, because the
     correct reduced-motion state here is the finished frame, not a zero-length
     animation that flashes. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .mesh-hero__status,
    .mesh-hero__title,
    .mesh-hero__who,
    .mesh-hero__actions {
      animation: mesh-rise var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out-expo) backwards;
    }

    .mesh-hero__title {
      animation-delay: 80ms;
    }
    .mesh-hero__who {
      animation-delay: 160ms;
    }
    .mesh-hero__actions {
      animation-delay: 240ms;
    }

    .mesh-hero__layer {
      animation: mesh-settle 900ms var(--ease-out-expo) backwards;
    }
  }

  @keyframes mesh-rise {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translate3d(0, 14px, 0);
    }
  }

  /* Opacity only. The layer's transform is the parallax offset and is written
     by script every frame, so animating transform here would be overwritten
     mid-flight the moment the pointer moves. */
  @keyframes mesh-settle {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The static state. The portrait is still the hero — it just does not
     follow the pointer. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .mesh-hero__layer {
      transform: none !important;
    }
  }

  /* -- Work index --------------------------------------------------------- */
  .index {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    border-block-start: var(--v2b-rule);
  }

  .index__row {
    border-block-end: var(--v2b-rule);
  }

  .index__link {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr auto;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-s);
    padding-block: var(--space-m);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--text);
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  @media (min-width: 42rem) {
    .index__link {
      grid-template-columns: 4rem 1fr 14rem 2rem;
      gap: var(--space-m);
    }
  }

  .index__n {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-synthesis: none;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  .index__title {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .index__cat {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /* Hidden below the wide breakpoint, where the grid drops to three columns
     and there is no cell for it. */
  .index__arrow {
    display: none;
    justify-self: end;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    transition:
      transform var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out-quart),
      color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  @media (min-width: 42rem) {
    .index__arrow {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  .index__link:hover,
  .index__link:focus-visible {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .index__link:hover .index__n,
  .index__link:focus-visible .index__n,
  .index__link:hover .index__arrow,
  .index__link:focus-visible .index__arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .index__link:hover .index__arrow,
  .index__link:focus-visible .index__arrow {
    transform: translateX(0.4em);
  }

  /* -- Hover preview ------------------------------------------------------ */
  /* position: fixed, not absolute — it is positioned from clientX/clientY,
     which are viewport coordinates. Absolute would need the list's offset
     parent subtracted on every move and would drift on scroll. */
  .index__preview {
    position: fixed;
    inset-block-start: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    inline-size: 16rem;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
    z-index: var(--z-raised);
    transition: opacity var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out-quart);
    transform: translate3d(-50%, -50%, 0);
  }

  .index__preview.is-visible {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .index__preview img {
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background-color: var(--bg-3);
  }

  /* No preview without a fine pointer, and none under reduced motion — an
     image that chases the cursor is motion, and it is not content. */
  @media (hover: none), (pointer: coarse), (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .index__preview {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { motionReduced } from '../../scripts/motion';

  /* =======================================================================
     HERO PARALLAX
     Ported from hero-parallax_6.html with the rect cached and the loop gated.
     ===================================================================== */
  (() => {
    const hero = document.getElementById('mesh-hero');
    if (!hero || motionReduced()) return;

    let tx = 0;
    let ty = 0;
    let x = 0;
    let y = 0;
    let raf = 0;
    let onScreen = true;
    let rect: DOMRect | null = null;

    /* 0.075 per frame, kept from the original because it was tuned by eye and
       looks right. It is frame-rate dependent — on a 120Hz display it settles
       about twice as fast — which is acceptable for an eased cosmetic offset
       and would not be for anything with a defined duration. */
    const EASE = 0.075;

    const refreshRect = () => {
      rect = hero.getBoundingClientRect();
    };

    const tick = () => {
      x += (tx - x) * EASE;
      y += (ty - y) * EASE;
      hero.style.setProperty('--mx', x.toFixed(4));
      hero.style.setProperty('--my', y.toFixed(4));

      if (Math.abs(tx - x) > 0.0005 || Math.abs(ty - y) > 0.0005) {
        raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      } else {
        /* Snap and stop. An idle page must not hold the compositor at 60fps
           to chase the last ten-thousandth of a unit. */
        x = tx;
        y = ty;
        hero.style.setProperty('--mx', x.toFixed(4));
        hero.style.setProperty('--my', y.toFixed(4));
        raf = 0;
      }
    };

    const wake = () => {
      if (!raf && onScreen) raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    };

    hero.addEventListener(
      'pointermove',
      (e) => {
        if (e.pointerType !== 'mouse') return;
        /* The rect is cached. The original measured it here, on every move —
           which forces a synchronous layout many times per frame. */
        if (!rect) refreshRect();
        if (!rect) return;
        tx = ((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * 2 - 1;
        ty = ((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * 2 - 1;
        wake();
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    hero.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => {
      tx = 0;
      ty = 0;
      wake();
    });

    window.addEventListener('scroll', refreshRect, { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener('resize', refreshRect, { passive: true });
    refreshRect();

    /* Never run while the hero is off screen. */
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        onScreen = entries.some((en) => en.isIntersecting);
        if (onScreen) {
          refreshRect();
          wake();
        } else if (raf) {
          cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
          raf = 0;
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0 },
    );
    io.observe(hero);

    /* Device tilt, for phones. NOT requested via
       DeviceOrientationEvent.requestPermission() — that needs a user gesture
       and a permission prompt, and a hero that asks for motion-sensor access
       before you have read a word is a worse trade than no tilt. So this
       simply works where the event fires unprompted (Android, older iOS) and
       is silently absent on modern iOS. */
    window.addEventListener('deviceorientation', (e) => {
      if (e.gamma == null || e.beta == null || motionReduced()) return;
      tx = Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, e.gamma / 25));
      ty = Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, (e.beta - 45) / 25));
      wake();
    });
  })();

  /* =======================================================================
     WORK INDEX — hover preview
     ===================================================================== */
  (() => {
    const list = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-index]');
    const pane = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-preview-pane]');
    const paneImg = pane?.querySelector('img');
    const fine = window.matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');
    if (!list || !pane || !paneImg || !fine.matches || motionReduced()) return;

    let raf = 0;
    let x = 0;
    let y = 0;
    let current = '';

    /* Writes happen once per frame rather than once per pointermove. A move
       handler that sets style directly runs far more often than the screen
       refreshes, and every write is a chance to force layout. */
    const flush = () => {
      raf = 0;
      pane.style.transform = `translate3d(${x}px, ${y}px, 0) translate(-50%, -50%)`;
    };

    list.addEventListener(
      'pointermove',
      (e) => {
        if (e.pointerType !== 'mouse') return;
        x = e.clientX + 120;
        y = e.clientY;
        if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(flush);
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    list.addEventListener(
      'pointerover',
      (e) => {
        const link = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLElement>('[data-preview]');
        if (!link) return;
        const src = link.dataset.preview || '';
        if (src && src !== current) {
          current = src;
          paneImg.src = src;
        }
        pane.classList.add('is-visible');
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    /* pointerleave on the LIST, not pointerout on each row — pointerout fires
       when moving between two rows and would flicker the preview off and on. */
    list.addEventListener(
      'pointerleave',
      () => {
        pane.classList.remove('is-visible');
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    /* Scrolling away from a hovered row leaves the preview stranded on
       screen, because no pointer event fires. */
    window.addEventListener(
      'scroll',
      () => {
        if (pane.classList.contains('is-visible')) pane.classList.remove('is-visible');
      },
      { passive: true },
    );
  })();
</script>
